00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.360
So my very first job as a rookie was to, as soon&nbsp;
as we got to miko was to get unstrapped out of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.880
seat, float down to a locker by me, get the&nbsp;
proper camera out, float up to the overhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.560
window while Dick Richards the commander on&nbsp;
my first flight, was pitching discovery over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:20.360
on its back so that we could take a picture of&nbsp;
this tank as it's following away back to earth.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.360
Well, I floated up to the overhead window and&nbsp;
I'm looking outside and Dick says, Hey Bruce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.360
you should be seeing it. And I said, yeah,&nbsp;
Dick, I do. It's awesome. It's beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.440
He said, well, why aren't you taking any&nbsp;
pictures? I said, pictures of what? Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:39.040
the tank. I was so blown away by seeing Earth&nbsp;
for the first time that I choked on my first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.120
job and I was supposed to have this great big&nbsp;
picture of the tank. Well, by the time I took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:45.400
a picture of the tank, it was like falling&nbsp;
away from us, but it was just beautiful. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:52.640
